! conclusion: Treat the issue trackers of constitutive upstream dependencies as stack-maintenance surfaces and walk only interventions grounded in deployment evidence maintainers can recognize as answering their questions.

  + context: War Room decision WR-23 (2026-07-17). Operator: Joe. The stack depends on upstream software as faculties: XTDB as memory, babashka as hands, FTS5 as recall, and A-Frame and troika as public sensorium.

  + IF:
    Upstream software constitutes the stack's core faculties, and the operator needs a cold outbound lane that reliably produces sends.

  + HOWEVER:
    Cold outreach to strangers decays while genuine deployment experience remains unused as evidence on live upstream design questions posed by maintainers.

  + THEN:
    Treat upstream trackers as stack surfaces. Stage deployment-evidence comments and maintainer-blessed small PRs on the M-cold-chain ladder, sent only by the operator, and record every rung's movement. Generate candidates by standing times liveness, dropping any candidate whose specific contribution cannot be stated truthfully.

  + BECAUSE:
    This lane is audience-first, leaves a public compounding artifact even without a response, makes the stack's own faculties more inhabitable, and offers maintainer merges as genuine external evaluation that cannot be self-supplied.

    + evidence: War Bulletin 13 records two read-only tracker sweeps on 2026-07-17 yielding about ten walkable rungs in three tiers. XTDB issue 5637 had already received a deployment-evidence comment on 2026-07-16; War Bulletin 14 records the maintainer reply on 2026-07-28, the first reply across either campaign.
